{
  "duration": 469.8480000000133,
  "chapters": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 95.76000000000273
    },
    {
      "start": 95.76000000000273,
      "end": 189.16800000000538
    },
    {
      "start": 189.16800000000538,
      "end": 279.93600000000794
    },
    {
      "start": 279.93600000000794,
      "end": 370.8960000000105
    },
    {
      "start": 370.8960000000105,
      "end": 469.8480000000133
    }
  ],
  "cues": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 1.2400000095367432,
      "text": "Hey, welcome back.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 2.0199999809265137,
      "end": 5.699999809265137,
      "text": "Imagine sending positively charged alpha particles toward an extremely thin",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 5.699999809265137,
      "end": 6.28000020980835,
      "text": "metal foil.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 7.300000190734863,
      "end": 9.260000228881836,
      "text": "Most pass through with little change in direction.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 9.819999694824219,
      "end": 14.680000305175781,
      "text": "A small fraction turn sharply, and a very few come back toward the source.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 15.479999542236328,
      "end": 17.540000915527344,
      "text": "Those rare events are the important clue.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 18.3799991607666,
      "end": 22.959999084472656,
      "text": "A diffuse positive charge spread throughout an atom cannot easily produce such",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 22.959999084472656,
      "end": 24.399999618530273,
      "text": "strong deflections in this picture.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 25.520000457763672,
      "end": 29.520000457763672,
      "text": "Rutherford's nuclear model puts the positive charge and almost all the mass",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 29.520000457763672,
      "end": 31.479999542236328,
      "text": "into a tiny central nucleus.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 32.68000030517578,
      "end": 35.52000045776367,
      "text": "Most of the atomic volume lies outside that nucleus.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 36.400001525878906,
      "end": 40.47999954223633,
      "text": "Please do not interpret the phrase mostly empty space as saying that quantum",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 40.47999954223633,
      "end": 42.959999084472656,
      "text": "electron distributions have no physical role.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 43.7400016784668,
      "end": 47.720001220703125,
      "text": "It is a comparison of the very small nuclear size with the much larger atomic",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 47.720001220703125,
      "end": 48.119998931884766,
      "text": "size.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 48.939998626708984,
      "end": 54.32461621211125,
      "text": "An alpha particle has charge plus two e, and a nucleus with atomic number Z",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 54.32461621211125,
      "end": 54.540000915527344,
      "text": "has",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 54.540000915527344,
      "end": 56.58000183105469,
      "text": "charge plus Z e.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 56.58000183105469,
      "end": 59.18000030517578,
      "text": "Their repulsion can bend the alpha particle's path.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 59.97999954223633,
      "end": 64.30000305175781,
      "text": "A particle passing far away feels a smaller deflecting influence than one",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 64.30000305175781,
      "end": 65.13999938964844,
      "text": "approaching closely.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 66.0999984741211,
      "end": 70.31999969482422,
      "text": "Think for a moment: which observation supports a small concentrated nucleus",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 70.31999969482422,
      "end": 71.04000091552734,
      "text": "most directly?",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 71.33999633789062,
      "end": 76.41999816894531,
      "text": "It is the rare large angle scattering, especially backward scattering, not",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 76.41999816894531,
      "end": 78.62000274658203,
      "text": "simply the fact that many particles pass through.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 79.19999694824219,
      "end": 82.33999633789062,
      "text": "The experiment changed the structure we assign to an atom.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 83,
      "end": 87.2000002738757,
      "text": "But a classical planetary model still faces a problem: an accelerating",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 87.2000002738757,
      "end": 87.68000030517578,
      "text": "orbiting",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 87.68000030517578,
      "end": 89.5,
      "text": "charge should radiate energy.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 90.69999694824219,
      "end": 94.72000122070312,
      "text": "Bohr's model will introduce new postulates to address atomic stability and",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 94.72000122070312,
      "end": 95.5199966430664,
      "text": "discrete spectra.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 95.76000000000273,
      "end": 99.29589748725186,
      "text": "Let's work out one clean scattering case rather than guessing from the",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 99.29589748725186,
      "end": 99.70000005722319,
      "text": "picture.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 100.41999984741484,
      "end": 104.44000030517851,
      "text": "An alpha particle approaches a heavy nucleus directly along the line joining",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 104.44000030517851,
      "end": 105.0600001907376,
      "text": "their centers.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 105.94000030517851,
      "end": 108.29999996185576,
      "text": "Treat the nucleus as fixed, neglecting recoil.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 109.19999958038603,
      "end": 113.05999923706328,
      "text": "At the closest point of this head on approach, the alpha particle momentarily",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 113.05999923706328,
      "end": 114.7800004577664,
      "text": "stops before moving back.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 115.41999984741484,
      "end": 119.46000076294219,
      "text": "Its initial kinetic energy has become electric potential energy of repulsion.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 120.21999908447539,
      "end": 125.35461553280443,
      "text": "Taking potential energy zero at infinite separation, write K equals k times",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 125.35461553280443,
      "end": 125.5600001907376,
      "text": "two",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 125.5600001907376,
      "end": 128.66000152588163,
      "text": "e times Z e divided by r minimum.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 130.00000167846952,
      "end": 134.32000137329374,
      "text": "Rearranging gives r minimum equal to two Z k e squared divided by K.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 135.07999969482694,
      "end": 137.4400003051785,
      "text": "The factor two comes from the alpha particle's charge.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 138.17999816894803,
      "end": 142.62000061035428,
      "text": "A useful constant is k e squared approximately one point four four mega",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 142.62000061035428,
      "end": 144.30000091553006,
      "text": "electron volts times femtometers.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 145.17999816894803,
      "end": 148.00000167846952,
      "text": "For a gold nucleus, Z is seventy nine.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 148.00000167846952,
      "end": 151.57999969482694,
      "text": "Let the incident alpha energy be seven point two mega electron volts.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 152.41999984741483,
      "end": 156.67999816894803,
      "text": "The distance is two times seventy nine times one point four four divided by",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 156.67999816894803,
      "end": 159.6999986267117,
      "text": "seven point two, giving thirty one point six femtometers.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 160.5399987792996,
      "end": 163.89999938965116,
      "text": "That is a center to center distance, not the alpha particle's traveled",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 163.89999938965116,
      "end": 164.38000274658475,
      "text": "distance.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 165.06000305176053,
      "end": 169.38000274658475,
      "text": "It is outside the approximate gold nuclear radius, so the Coulomb model is",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 169.38000274658475,
      "end": 170.5399987792996,
      "text": "sensible for this illustration.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 171.5200021362332,
      "end": 175.8599984741238,
      "text": "Your turn: if incident energy doubles, what happens to this head on closest",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 175.8599984741238,
      "end": 176.21999908447538,
      "text": "distance?",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 176.77999664306913,
      "end": 177.49999786377225,
      "text": "It halves.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 178.2400033569363,
      "end": 181.9599969482449,
      "text": "For a non head on trajectory, motion has an angular component at closest",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 181.9599969482449,
      "end": 185.26000000000272,
      "text": "approach, so you cannot say all kinetic energy has vanished.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 186.06000305176053,
      "end": 188.80000091553006,
      "text": "Use the head on formula only when that condition is stated.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 189.16800000000538,
      "end": 193.24492285684977,
      "text": "Bohr kept a nuclear center but added rules that classical motion alone does",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 193.24492285684977,
      "end": 193.40799977112354,
      "text": "not",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 193.40799977112354,
      "end": 193.7079999618584,
      "text": "supply.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 194.60800005722584,
      "end": 197.9280002288872,
      "text": "Certain stationary states are allowed, and an electron in one of those states",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 197.9280002288872,
      "end": 200.2680003814751,
      "text": "does not continuously radiate energy in the model.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 201.06799961853565,
      "end": 206.3279998474175,
      "text": "The angular momentum is restricted to n times h divided by two pi, with n a",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 206.3279998474175,
      "end": 206.96799923706592,
      "text": "positive integer.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 208.12799908447803,
      "end": 211.22799946594776,
      "text": "Combining that postulate with Coulomb force providing the centripetal force",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 211.22799946594776,
      "end": 213.1479995422417,
      "text": "gives allowed radii and energies.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 214.3279998474175,
      "end": 218.2774863842749,
      "text": "For a hydrogen like system containing exactly one electron, the energy is",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 218.2774863842749,
      "end": 218.54799916077198,
      "text": "minus",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 218.54799916077198,
      "end": 222.8880012207085,
      "text": "thirteen point six times Z squared divided by n squared, in electron volts.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 224.1479995422417,
      "end": 228.56800152588428,
      "text": "The radius is zero point zero five two nine times n squared divided by Z, in",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 228.56800152588428,
      "end": 228.96799923706592,
      "text": "nanometers.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 229.76799847412647,
      "end": 232.70800091553272,
      "text": "We neglect reduced mass corrections in these introductory formulas.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 233.94799877930225,
      "end": 237.70800091553272,
      "text": "Negative energy means the electron is bound, with the zero chosen for a",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 237.70800091553272,
      "end": 238.6479995422417,
      "text": "separated electron and nucleus.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 239.6479995422417,
      "end": 241.36800076294483,
      "text": "It does not mean negative kinetic energy.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 242.36800076294483,
      "end": 244.00800015259327,
      "text": "For hydrogen, Z is one.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 244.62799908447803,
      "end": 248.80799938965382,
      "text": "At n equal to two, energy is minus three point four electron volts, and radius",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 248.80799938965382,
      "end": 250.9879996948296,
      "text": "is zero point two one one six nanometers.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 252.42799832153858,
      "end": 256.16800000000535,
      "text": "Compare helium plus, which also has one electron but Z equal to two.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 256.9479987793022,
      "end": 260.14800335693894,
      "text": "Its ground state energy is minus fifty four point four electron volts.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 260.7880027465874,
      "end": 263.5479972534233,
      "text": "The stronger nuclear attraction makes it more tightly bound.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 264.6080024414116,
      "end": 267.76799847412644,
      "text": "Do not apply these simple formulas to neutral helium with two electrons.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 268.8079993896538,
      "end": 272.7279975585991,
      "text": "Also remember that Bohr orbits are a historical model, not the full modern",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 272.7279975585991,
      "end": 274.3880012207085,
      "text": "quantum description of electron position.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 275.24800183106004,
      "end": 278.58799816895066,
      "text": "The model's useful predictions here are its energy levels and the resulting",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 278.58799816895066,
      "end": 279.4079978637749,
      "text": "spectral frequencies.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 279.93600000000794,
      "end": 281.09599996662934,
      "text": "Now we have levels.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 281.8960000381549,
      "end": 283.2159999713977,
      "text": "How does light enter the story?",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 283.93600000000794,
      "end": 287.8480515676724,
      "text": "An electron making a transition from a higher energy level to a lower one",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 287.8480515676724,
      "end": 288.1160003051837,
      "text": "emits",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 288.1160003051837,
      "end": 289.7159997329791,
      "text": "a photon carrying the difference.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 290.6359998092731,
      "end": 294.09599984742005,
      "text": "Initial energy minus final energy is positive for that downward transition.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 294.8560000763019,
      "end": 298.81599916077454,
      "text": "Do not subtract the magnitudes blindly; write the signed energies first.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 299.4760009155353,
      "end": 303.743692405513,
      "text": "For hydrogen moving from n equal to three to n equal to two, energy released",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 303.743692405513,
      "end": 303.8560000763019,
      "text": "is",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 303.8560000763019,
      "end": 307.4959994659503,
      "text": "thirteen point six times one fourth minus one ninth.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 307.4959994659503,
      "end": 310.55600083924134,
      "text": "That is approximately one point eight eight nine electron volts.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 311.6759997711261,
      "end": 315.83600152588684,
      "text": "Using h c approximately twelve hundred forty electron volt nanometers, divide",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 315.83600152588684,
      "end": 317.49600137329895,
      "text": "twelve hundred forty by that energy.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 318.03599847412903,
      "end": 321.8759986267169,
      "text": "The wavelength is about six hundred fifty six nanometers, a visible Balmer",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 321.8759986267169,
      "end": 322.2159987793048,
      "text": "line.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 322.8759986267169,
      "end": 324.99600137329895,
      "text": "Let's compare the transition from two to one.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 325.5160018310626,
      "end": 329.6759997711261,
      "text": "Its photon energy is ten point two electron volts, giving roughly one hundred",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 329.6759997711261,
      "end": 331.59599984742005,
      "text": "twenty two nanometers in the ultraviolet.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 332.15600122071106,
      "end": 334.8959990844806,
      "text": "Larger energy difference means shorter photon wavelength.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 335.69599832154114,
      "end": 339.75599969483216,
      "text": "Pause for a prediction: does an electron at n equal to two need thirteen point",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 339.75599969483216,
      "end": 342.35599816895325,
      "text": "six electron volts to be removed completely?",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 343.15600122071106,
      "end": 343.49600137329895,
      "text": "No.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 343.9959975586017,
      "end": 347.83600152588684,
      "text": "Its energy is minus three point four, so reaching zero requires three point",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 347.83600152588684,
      "end": 349.03599847412903,
      "text": "four electron volts.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 349.03599847412903,
      "end": 353.53599847412903,
      "text": "Thirteen point six is the ground state ionization energy for hydrogen in this",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 353.53599847412903,
      "end": 353.75599969483216,
      "text": "model.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 354.41600335694153,
      "end": 358.17599786377747,
      "text": "For absorption between discrete bound states, the photon must match an allowed",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 358.17599786377747,
      "end": 360.5759993896564,
      "text": "energy gap, subject to transition rules.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 361.33600152588684,
      "end": 365.55600274658997,
      "text": "Ionization is different: a photon above the ionization threshold can leave the",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 365.55600274658997,
      "end": 367.65600122071106,
      "text": "released electron with surplus kinetic energy.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 368.5160018310626,
      "end": 370.6359969482501,
      "text": "Identify the starting state before choosing a threshold.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 370.8960000000105,
      "end": 374.85600003815745,
      "text": "Let's organize the spectrum instead of memorizing isolated colors.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 375.51599988556956,
      "end": 379.7559996566877,
      "text": "A spectral series groups transitions that finish at the same lower level.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 380.63599977112864,
      "end": 384.23600015259836,
      "text": "Lyman lines finish at one and lie in the ultraviolet for hydrogen.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 385.2159996948347,
      "end": 386.6160002670393,
      "text": "Balmer lines finish at two.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 387.3960000000105,
      "end": 390.6560002288923,
      "text": "Paschen lines finish at three and lie in the infrared for hydrogen.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 391.3160000763044,
      "end": 397.0160008392439,
      "text": "The Rydberg relation gives inverse wavelength as R H times Z squared times the",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 397.0160008392439,
      "end": 400.13599977112864,
      "text": "difference between inverse squared lower and higher quantum numbers.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 400.695999237071,
      "end": 403.73600015259836,
      "text": "Use the lower index first so the bracket is positive.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 404.47600183106516,
      "end": 408.23600015259836,
      "text": "In a given emission series, increasing the starting level makes the photon",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 408.23600015259836,
      "end": 410.0559998474226,
      "text": "energy approach a limiting value.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 410.73600015259836,
      "end": 414.23600015259836,
      "text": "The shortest wavelength is the series limit, not the first line.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 414.85599908448313,
      "end": 419.2960015258894,
      "text": "For hydrogen's Balmer limit, the photon energy approaches three point four",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 419.2960015258894,
      "end": 423.8960000000105,
      "text": "electron volts, giving roughly three hundred sixty five nanometers with our",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 423.8960000000105,
      "end": 424.3960000000105,
      "text": "rounded constants.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 425.0960007629499,
      "end": 430.03830786838114,
      "text": "Now a different kind of JEE question: an ensemble of excited hydrogen atoms",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 430.03830786838114,
      "end": 430.23600015259836,
      "text": "can",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 430.23600015259836,
      "end": 432.73600015259836,
      "text": "make transitions among levels one through four.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 433.4560013733015,
      "end": 435.65600213624094,
      "text": "How many distinct energy differences are possible?",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 436.5160027465925,
      "end": 436.9159966430769,
      "text": "Count pairs.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 437.47600183106516,
      "end": 440.1160012207136,
      "text": "Four times three divided by two gives six.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 440.7359963379011,
      "end": 444.97600183106516,
      "text": "That does not mean one electron emits all six photons during a single downward",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 444.97600183106516,
      "end": 445.4360009155378,
      "text": "cascade.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 445.9559975586042,
      "end": 450.5359993896589,
      "text": "An individual cascade follows one route and can contain at most three downward",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 450.5359993896589,
      "end": 452.2159996948347,
      "text": "steps from four to one.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 452.9360009155378,
      "end": 456.5588175393234,
      "text": "Across many atoms and different routes, the six transition energies can",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 456.5588175393234,
      "end": 456.9159966430769,
      "text": "appear.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 457.13599786378,
      "end": 461.57600030518626,
      "text": "More detailed quantum transition selection rules are outside this simple",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 461.57600030518626,
      "end": 462.47600183106516,
      "text": "counting exercise.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 463.3960000000105,
      "end": 467.05600366211985,
      "text": "Finish by checking the lower series level, the energy sign, and whether a",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 467.05600366211985,
      "end": 469.65600213624094,
      "text": "question refers to one cascade or an ensemble.",
      "chapter": 4
    }
  ]
}
